karadoc wrote:
> I have considered a using a non-linear algorithm, and I've even
> thought of an ad hoc justification for doing so: the nethack 'weight'
> of things could represent how hard it is to carry something rather
> than just how much it weighs. For example, it might take a lot of care
> to carry 20 rings compared to one sword. So the actual weight might be
> a bit less.

This goes back to early editions of D&D which Nethack derives a lot of
concepts from. D&D encumbrance encompasses both weight and bulk. I still
remember the quote from one of the game manuals:

"Players looking at this table will complain: 'But a scroll doesn't
weigh two pounds!'"

Notionally, 10 gold pieces in D&D weigh one pound (.454 kg if that's
your preferred unit), and I'm pretty sure that Nethack weight units are
the same or very similar.

The ability to carry something doesn't depend strictly on its
weight/mass. My infant daughter weighs the same as a large sack of cat
food. The baby is more ergonomic and is a lot easier to carry. :)
However, they'd both be listed as 10kg in the hypothetical RLRL (Real
Life Roguelike).

So doing the direct translation of Nethack item weights to "real" units
for display purposes may not be all that feasible, or at least believable.


